//! Batch 357 — the moeshift Apple-MoE-serving `kernel_opt` distill
//! (Plan 367 / Research 401): two host-orchestration/cache-policy rules —
//! the readiness-partitioned expert submit and the protected-tier
//! score-gated admission — plus two count-neutral rider faces on their
//! OWNERS (`metal-keepalive-residency-unwire` in `rust_perf` `entries_21` and
//! `decayed-frequency-eviction-recency-tiebreak-route-protection` here).
//!
//! Source: `davide-troiani/moeshift` @
//! `2b1b3e87ea94059518179bd93252c7452d6c11f3` (develop tip 2026-11-12, MIT;
//! llama.cpp-omni fork — the LES/SSD foundations are tc-mb upstream's, the
//! Qwen runtime / Metal scheduling / cache work the fork's; cite BOTH).
//! Per the transcription law the corpus consumes DECISION CONTENT with an
//! ORIGINAL Rust fixture (`corpus/fixtures/b357_fixtures.rs`, 9/9
//! standalone-compiled AND executed before entry), never source verbatim —
//! the Metal/C++ surface is modeled as a mock dispatch layer; the fixture
//! never calls a real GPU. Every quote below byte-verified at the pin
//! (tip unchanged at re-clone; T0 by pin identity + spot greps). Batch 357
//! / Plan 367 / Research 401.

use crate::domains::kernel_opt::corpus::KernelRule;

pub fn entries() -> Vec<KernelRule> {
    vec![
        // ── K1 — the readiness-partitioned expert submit: resident work
        // submits while the reads fill; pending ids travel as negative
        // sentinels; the phase rides the sign of one scalar arg; the
        // first threadgroup zero-fills the inactive column; pending bytes
        // are never consumed as ready data ─────────────────────────────
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "readiness-partitioned-expert-resident-missing-submit".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "MECHANISM (readiness split of MoE expert work across a streaming boundary; category H — MoE dispatch, host-orchestration): a MoE serving layer whose selected experts' weights may NOT all be resident at submit time (SSD/host-tier streaming, an LRU bank mid-fill) must not serialize the token behind the slowest read — split the selected experts by READINESS and submit TWO passes of the SAME kernel: RESIDENT experts compute NOW (overlapping the fill), MISSING experts gate OFF and compute in a second pass dispatched only after the demand-read completion boundary. THE FOUR LAWS. (1) READINESS LIVES IN THE DATA, not a side channel: expert ids in the id buffer carry negative sentinels for pending slots — verbatim (at the pin, ggml/src/ggml-metal/ggml-metal.metal, kernel_mul_mv_id): 'const int phase = args.nei1 < 0 ? -args.nei1 : GGML_MOE_PHASE_ALL;' + 'const bool pending = i02 < 0;' + 'if (pending) i02 = -i02 - 1;' — the kernel recovers the real id from the negated form and gates itself per expert with zero host round-trip per element. (2) THE PHASE RIDES THE SIGN of one existing scalar arg (the line above: nei1 negative = gated mode, its magnitude the phase; positive = the ordinary ALL path) — no new kernel-args field, no extra buffer binding, no dispatch specialization; the phase vocabulary is shared scheduling↔execution (GGML_MOE_PHASE_ALL/RESIDENT/MISSING in ggml-metal-impl.h). (3) MISMATCHED-PHASE THREADGROUPS EARLY-EXIT AND THE FIRST GROUP ZERO-FILLS THE INACTIVE COLUMN: verbatim 'if ((phase == GGML_MOE_PHASE_RESIDENT && pending) || (phase == GGML_MOE_PHASE_MISSING && !pending)) { // One threadgroup clears the inactive column. Other groups do no work.' — outputs stay well-defined without a separate clear pass and without a zero-initialized buffer (the zero-init-rides-an-existing-pass idiom — gpu_18's counters zero-filled riding the same pass is the kinship; cite, never re-own). Each pass writes its own columns and zeroes the rest; the combine lands every column exactly once before routing-weight reduction. (4) THE BOUNDARY INVARIANT: verbatim (docs/architecture.md §9) 'Pending expert bytes are not consumed as ready data.' — pending slots are never computed in the resident phase; the missing pass dispatches only after the reads complete; the router stays authoritative (verbatim, docs/les/adaptive-prefill.md: 'Predictions schedule reads; the actual router determines which experts are computed.') — lookahead only schedules reads and biases cache retention, never replaces selected experts. REGIME: MoE serving with streaming/offloaded expert weights where a SUBSET of selected experts is resident at submit time (SSD tier, host-resident tier, LRU bank mid-fill); any graph whose per-element work can gate on a data-carried readiness flag. ANTI-REGIME: weight-complete models (all-resident serving — no split exists, the phase arg stays ALL and the gated path compiles away); discrete inference; engines where the scheduler CANNOT know readiness at submit (that is the optimistic pole's world, see the adjudication). MANDATORY ADJUDICATION BOTH DIRECTIONS vs compute-experts-before-router-lookup-fixup (B337, jitengine): THAT is the OPTIMISTIC pole — one submission against a device-side slot snapshot, the host discards snapshot lies and rebuilds only the missing rows, hiding HOST lookup latency with post-hoc fixup; THIS is the KNOWN-PENDING pole — the scheduler owns the reads so it KNOWS which slots are pending, gates them off, and hides SSD/read latency with a boundary-split second pass. Two poles of one readiness-trust axis: jitengine trusts-then-verifies (one launch, wasted compute on a lie, no second dispatch); moeshift knows-and-gates (two launches + a completion wait, zero wasted compute). They COMPOSE (snapshot trust where lookups race the submit; sentinel gating where I/O is scheduler-visible), never merge. Distinct-from expert-loop-two-phase-split-batch-across-iterations (gpu_05: loop STAGE halves around helper-encapsulated syncs — THIS is readiness halves around scheduler-visible I/O), from gpu-resident-moe-routing-no-host-sync (B18: the routing PIPELINE goes device-side to avoid readbacks — THIS keeps the scheduler host-side on purpose because it owns the file reads), and from expert-cache-write-path-regime-gated (B79: gates the cache WRITE path by regime — THIS gates KERNEL work by per-slot readiness; orthogonal, composable with any of those caches as the readiness source). PORTABLE as a readiness law: any dispatched work over a slot map with a pending/filled distinction can carry readiness in the data, gate per work-item, and boundary-split the pass. NotBenchable here (the win is hidden read/SSD latency under a serving queue; no validator hosts that — the dial9/136 annotation, not a demotion); the fixture proves gating, sentinel recovery, zero-fill and submit ORDER. Source: davide-troiani/moeshift (MIT) @ 2b1b3e87ea94059518179bd93252c7452d6c11f3 — ggml/src/ggml-metal/ggml-metal.metal (kernel_mul_mv_id phase block, quoted above), ggml/src/ggml-metal/ggml-metal-impl.h (the phase enum), src/llama-moe-slot.cpp (slot resolution + reader ownership: 'A slot claimed by a reader is pending and cannot be evicted. Its mapping is published only after its bytes have been written.' — docs/architecture.md §5), docs/architecture.md §5/§9 (the resident/missing sequence + the boundary invariant), docs/les/adaptive-prefill.md (the phase identifiers + the router-authority line). Provenance: the resident/missing path is the fork's Metal scheduling work on tc-mb's LES foundation — cite both. Batch 357 / Plan 367 / Research 401.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// naive: the token waits for the slowest expert read — every\n// selected expert's weights are awaited before ANY expert computes;\n// resident work sits idle behind the fill. Readiness, if tracked at\n// all, lives in a host-side set the kernel cannot see.\nfn submit_naive(selected: &[(u32, bool)], slots: &mut Slots, io: &Io) -> Vec<f32> {\n    let mut ready = Vec::new();\n    for (expert, resident) in selected {\n        if !resident {\n            io.wait_for(*expert); // the token blocks on the slowest read\n        }\n        ready.push(slots.device_ptr(*expert));\n    }\n    io.wait_all();\n    dispatch_experts(&ready) // one pass, serialized behind the fill\n}\n\nstruct Slots;\n\nimpl Slots {\n    fn device_ptr(&self, _e: u32) -> *const f32 {\n        std::ptr::null()\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Io;\n\nimpl Io {\n    fn wait_for(&self, _e: u32) {}\n    fn wait_all(&self) {}\n}\n\nfn dispatch_experts(_p: &[*const f32]) -> Vec<f32> {\n    Vec::new()\n}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// readiness-partitioned: pending ids travel as negative sentinels\n// in the id buffer; the phase rides the sign of one scalar arg; the\n// resident pass submits now, the missing pass only after the reads\n// complete; each pass zero-fills the columns it does not own.\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\nenum Readiness {\n    Resident(i32),\n    /// payload = -(real_id) - 1; the kernel recovers the id on use.\n    Pending(i32),\n}\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Debug)]\nenum Phase {\n    All,\n    Resident,\n    Missing,\n}\n\nfn decode_phase(arg: i32) -> Phase {\n    if arg >= 0 {\n        Phase::All\n    } else {\n        match -arg {\n            1 => Phase::Resident,\n            2 => Phase::Missing,\n            _ => unreachable!(),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy)]\nstruct Selected {\n    expert: u32,\n    slot: Readiness,\n    order: usize,\n}\n\n/// One pass. Returns the inactive columns (zero-filled by the first\n/// group). A pending id computed as ready is a LOUD canary, never silent.\nfn submit_phase(\n    selected: &[Selected],\n    phase_arg: i32,\n    fill_complete: bool,\n    resident_out: &mut Vec<(u32, usize)>,\n    missing_out: &mut Vec<(u32, usize)>,\n    zeroed: &mut Vec<usize>,\n    pending_early: &mut Vec<u32>,\n) -> Vec<usize> {\n    let phase = decode_phase(phase_arg);\n    let mut inactive = Vec::new();\n    for pos in selected {\n        match (phase, pos.slot) {\n            (Phase::Resident, Readiness::Resident(_)) => resident_out.push((pos.expert, pos.order)),\n            (Phase::Resident, Readiness::Pending(_)) => {\n                inactive.push(pos.order);\n                zeroed.push(pos.order);\n            }\n            (Phase::Missing, Readiness::Pending(_)) if !fill_complete => {\n                // the boundary violation the fixture's canary exists for\n                pending_early.push(pos.expert);\n            }\n            (Phase::Missing, Readiness::Pending(_)) => missing_out.push((pos.expert, pos.order)),\n            (Phase::Missing, Readiness::Resident(_)) => {\n                inactive.push(pos.order);\n                zeroed.push(pos.order);\n            }\n            (Phase::All, _) => resident_out.push((pos.expert, pos.order)),\n        }\n    }\n    inactive\n}\n\n// token step: resident pass at -1 now; the missing pass at -2 only\n// after the demand-read completion; the combine lands every column\n// exactly once in router order.\n".to_string(),
        },
        // ── K2 — the protected-tier score-gated admission: a recent victim
        // demotes into protected only if its score beats the weakest
        // protected entry; refusal reuses the recent slot in place;
        // promotion is a metadata flip, bytes never move ────────────────
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "protected-tier-score-gated-admission".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "MECHANISM (two-tier cache ADMISSION gate; category A — memory budgeting, cache policy): a two-group cache sharing ONE physical slot set (recent + protected logical groups over the same buffers) must gate PROMOTION into the protected tier by SCORE COMPARISON, not by fill events — a recent victim demotes into protected only if its decayed demand score is at least the weakest eligible protected entry's; on refusal the recent slot is REUSED IN PLACE (nothing enters protected, nothing is evicted from it). THE LAWS. (1) THE GATE, verbatim (at the pin, src/llama-moe-protected-cache.h, miss()): 'if (cold >= 0 && frequency[ids[old]] >= frequency[ids[cold]]) { group[old] = 2; group[cold] = 1; ++admissions; return cold; } return old;' — the recent victim (old) demotes and the weakest protected entry (cold) is evicted ONLY when the candidate is at least as strong; otherwise the function returns the victim's own slot: the admission silently refused, the slot reused, the protected tier structurally untouched. (2) LOGICAL OWNERSHIP NEVER MOVES BYTES: verbatim (header line 28) 'Logical ownership changes; expert bytes and tensor addresses never move.' — hit/miss promote and demote by flipping group membership (group[slot] = 1/2); only a genuine eviction + refill writes bytes; the same physical allocation serves both tiers. (3) THE SCORING IS DECAYED DEMAND WITH A CALIBRATION KNOB: verbatim (begin_step) 'frequency[e] = decay * frequency[e] + (requested[e] ? 1.0f : 0.0f);' with the constant CARRIED AS AN ADMITTED SHORTCUT — 'constexpr float decay = 0.9785720621f; // 2^(-1/32)' beside 'shortcut: fixed 32-token half-life, calibrate with routing traces if workload performance requires it.' — the half-life is explicitly NOT a measured optimum; a fixture or port must carry it as a knob, never a magic number. (4) VICTIM SELECTION EXCLUDES LIVE WORK AND SOFTENS ON PREDICTIONS: victims exclude requested experts and pending reads (ownership pins) and prefer avoiding predicted experts — verbatim (victim()): two passes, pass 0 skips '!requested.empty() && requested[e]' and predicted entries, pass 1 admits them as fallback; protected victims rank by 'frequency[e] < frequency[ids[best]]' with age breaking ties. THE BOUNDARY vs THE B94 FAMILY (the mandatory adjudication): decayed-frequency-eviction-recency-tiebreak-route-protection (entries_gpu_10) owns the SINGLE-TIER victim policy — decayed hotness, hits+misses both vote, route protection, recency tiebreak, fail-loud when all candidates are protected. THE CORE SCORING HERE IS A POSITIVE INSTANCE OF THAT RULE (the decay, the vote semantics, the protection, the age tiebreak all re-derive it — this entry does NOT claim them). THE UNOWNED AXES this entry claims: (a) the TWO-TIER ADMISSION GATE — insertion into the hot tier is a score comparison against the weaker tier's minimum, and refusal reuses in place instead of forcing admission (LRFU's insertion policy, distinct from every single-tier victim choice and from regime-START gates); (b) the METADATA-FLIP ECONOMY — promotion/demotion between logical groups over one physical set moves ZERO bytes, so the tiers share slots without copy traffic. Distinct-from fill-gate-speculation-until-working-set-resident (gates a speculative REGIME on fill state at regime start — THIS gates each ADMISSION by score), from scratch-bank-isolates-one-shot-operands-from-hot-cache (region isolation via a SECOND physical bank — THIS is two logical groups over ONE physical set), from expert-cache-write-path-regime-gated (gates the WRITE path by traffic REGIME — THIS gates tier ADMISSION by score), and from tier-hit-refreshes-lru-recency-at-owning-tier (cross-tier recency COHERENCE on hits — THIS is the admission decision on misses; compose: that law governs how hits refresh, this governs whether a miss may enter). REGIME: fixed-capacity resident caches for routed operands (expert weights, adapters) where a transient cold burst must not evict the hot tier — any two-tier LRU/LFU hybrid. ANTI-REGIME: single-tier caches (no gate exists — use the B94 victim policy directly); caches whose protected tier is write-once; workloads with no locality (the gate's bookkeeping buys nothing). NotBenchable here (the win metric is hot-set retention / hit-rate on route traces — B94's own declared bench axis — not validator latency/alloc; the dial9/136 annotation). DROP-ALLOWED at this grade: the gate + the no-move economy are the whole claim; if the coordinator falsifies the gate's novelty against an owned two-tier rule, the honest landing is demote-to-face on B94's entry. Source: davide-troiani/moeshift (MIT) @ 2b1b3e87ea94059518179bd93252c7452d6c11f3 — src/llama-moe-protected-cache.h in full (the struct, begin_step, victim, hit, miss — quoted above), src/llama-moe-slot.cpp (the phase integration: 'Pending reads pin their slots until publication.' — docs/les/protected-decode-cache.md slot-ownership section), docs/les/protected-decode-cache.md (the demand-LRFU doc: the score equation, the vote semantics, the half-life disclosure). Provenance: LRFU (Lee, Bong, Shin — the recency-weighted-frequency paper the source cites) transcribed through the fork's protected-cache header; the DECAYED-DEMAND core is B94's DwarfStar-lineage law — cite both, claim only the gate. Batch 357 / Plan 367 / Research 401.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// naive: single-tier decayed-LRU — every miss takes the min-score\n// slot regardless of what that slot protects. A cold burst walks the\n// hot tier out slot by slot; the burst's own entries then age out and\n// the hot set is gone.\nstruct HotCache {\n    slots: Vec<Option<(u32, f32)>>, // (expert, score)\n}\n\nimpl HotCache {\n    fn miss_naive(&mut self, expert: u32, score: f32) -> usize {\n        let victim = self\n            .slots\n            .iter()\n            .enumerate()\n            .min_by(|a, b| a.1.unwrap().1.total_cmp(&b.1.unwrap().1))\n            .expect(\"slots\")\n            .0;\n        self.slots[victim] = Some((expert, score)); // cold bursts win\n        victim\n    }\n}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// two-tier, one physical set: the gate compares the candidate with\n// the weakest protected entry; refusal reuses the recent slot in\n// place; promotion/demotion are metadata flips (zero bytes moved).\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Debug)]\nenum Group {\n    Free,\n    Recent,\n    Protected,\n}\n\nstruct TieredCache {\n    group: Vec<Group>,\n    ages: Vec<u64>,\n    frequency: Vec<f32>, // decayed demand; the half-life is a knob\n}\n\nimpl TieredCache {\n    /// No free slot: the victim demotes ONLY through the gate.\n    fn admit_or_reuse(\n        &mut self,\n        expert: u32,\n        occupants: &mut [Option<u32>],\n    ) -> (usize, bool) {\n        let victim = self.oldest_recent();\n        let candidate_score = self.frequency[expert as usize];\n        if let Some(cold) = self.weakest_protected(occupants) {\n            let cold_score = self.frequency[occupants[cold].expect(\"occupant\") as usize];\n            if candidate_score >= cold_score {\n                // admitted: metadata flips; only the cold slot refills\n                self.group[victim] = Group::Protected;\n                self.group[cold] = Group::Recent;\n                occupants[cold] = Some(expert);\n                return (cold, true);\n            }\n        }\n        // REFUSED: reuse the recent slot in place — the protected tier is\n        // structurally untouched.\n        occupants[victim] = Some(expert);\n        (victim, false)\n    }\n\n    fn oldest_recent(&self) -> usize {\n        self.group\n            .iter()\n            .enumerate()\n            .filter(|(_, g)| **g == Group::Recent)\n            .min_by_key(|(i, _)| self.ages[*i])\n            .map(|(i, _)| i)\n            .expect(\"recent slot\")\n    }\n\n    fn weakest_protected(&self, occupants: &[Option<u32>]) -> Option<usize> {\n        let mut best: Option<(f32, usize)> = None;\n        for (i, g) in self.group.iter().enumerate() {\n            if *g != Group::Protected {\n                continue;\n            }\n            let f = self.frequency[occupants[i]? as usize];\n            match best {\n                Some((bf, _)) if f > bf => {}\n                _ => best = Some((f, i)),\n            }\n        }\n        best.map(|(_, i)| i)\n    }\n}\n".to_string(),
        },
    ]
}
